<!--
  Lab segment: real layer 2 between guests, no way out.

  There is no <forward> element, which is what makes the network isolated —
  libvirt installs rules that drop anything trying to leave the bridge. There
  IS an <ip>, so the host holds 10.10.10.1 and dnsmasq serves DHCP: targets on
  this network are reachable from the host, which is what lets a browser here
  open a web app running on a guest.

  Everything a class exercise needs from a switch works, because virbr-lab is
  one: ARP is real, broadcast and multicast reach every port, and a guest in
  promiscuous mode sees its neighbours' frames.

  Define once (this is libvirt state, not something the rebuild manages):

    sudo virsh net-define /etc/libvirt-networks/lab.xml
    sudo virsh net-start lab
    sudo virsh net-autostart lab

  10.10.10.0/24 is deliberately nothing like 192.168.100.0/24 (the house LAN)
  or 192.168.122.0/24 (libvirt's own NAT net), so a wrong-subnet mistake during
  an exercise is obvious rather than ambiguous.
-->
<network>
  <name>lab</name>
  <bridge name='virbr-lab' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <ip address='10.10.10.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <range start='10.10.10.100' end='10.10.10.200'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>
</network>
